.TH LORACHK 1
.SH NAME
lorachk \- check the lora link layer
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B lorachk setup
.I addr
.br
.B lorachk send
.I to text
.RI [ count ]
.br
.B lorachk ack
.I to text
.RI [ count ]
.br
.B lorachk elsewhere
.I to
.br
.B lorachk recv
.RI [ count ]
.br
.B lorachk ackrecv
.RI [ count ]
.br
.B lorachk stats
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Lorachk
exercises
.IR lora (3)
\- the addressing, acknowledgement and retry taken out of the packet
device \- from outside the kernel, where a shell cannot reach.
.PP
.B Setup
attaches
.B /dev/radio0/data
to
.B #L
and sets this station's address, then checks four things that need no
second station: that a conversation's
.B iounit
is the radio's less the four header bytes, that a write with nobody to
send to is refused, that
.B connect
is accepted, and that a write past the payload is refused naming the
budget.
.PP
.B Send
and
.B recv
are one unacknowledged packet each way;
.B ack
and
.B ackrecv
the same with acknowledgement on.  With a
.I count
they repeat, which is what makes a lossy channel say anything: one
packet either arrives or does not, and a run of them measures.
.PP
.B Elsewhere
sends a packet addressed to somebody else.  The other station must
count it under
.B notours
and deliver nothing, and without that counter a working address filter
and a dead link look exactly the same from the ground.
.PP
.B Stats
prints the interface's counters.
.SH EXAMPLE
Two stations under a QEMU patched to model an SX1276, one listening and
one connecting.  On the first:
.PP
.EX
	lorachk setup 2
	lorachk ackrecv 4
.EE
.PP
and on the second:
.PP
.EX
	lorachk setup 1
	lorachk ack 2 loss 8
.EE
.SH SOURCE
.B /appl/cmd/lorachk.b
.SH SEE ALSO
.I lora (3),
.I loraraw (1),
.I radiochk (1),
.I radio (3)
.SH BUGS
Each invocation opens its own conversation and closes it on exit, so
counters in a conversation's
.B status
cover that invocation and the interface's
.B stats
cover the boot.
.PP
.B Setup
must run before anything else, and only once: a second
.B "fd n"
is refused.
